<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Consistori del Gai Saber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1323">1323</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Consistory of the Gay Science</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Consistori del Gai Saber ('Consistory of the Gay Science') was a literary society founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 1323 by seven citizens to preserve the poetry of the troubadours. It held a yearly poetry contest with prizes of golden flowers, the Jeux floraux, and its Leys d'amors set out rules of Occitan grammar and verse. It survives today as the Académie des Jeux floraux.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/consistori-del-gai-saber.jpg" alt="Consistori del Gai Saber" width="198" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leys_d%27amor,_initial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The seven founders, literary men of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, set out their aims in a manifesto in Old Occitan verse, pledging prizes for poetry in the manner and language of the classical troubadours of roughly 1160 to 1220. The academy was first called the Consistori dels Sept Trobadors, the Consistory of the Seven Troubadours. It was governed by a chancellor and seven judges known as mantenedors, or maintainers. In 1390 John I of Aragon founded the Consistori de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in imitation of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>At the yearly contest the most excellent poet received the violeta d'aur, the golden violet, for the best poem or cançó. Other forms had other flowers: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marigold for the best dança and a silver dog rose for the best sirventés, pastorèla or vergièra. The first prize went on 3 May 1324 to Arnaut Vidal de Castelnou d'Ari for a sirventes in praise of the Virgin Mary, and the last of this early period in 1484 to Arnaut Bernart de Tarascon. Records of around a hundred prizes survive, won by men and women from both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>.</p><p>To judge these contests the Consistori commissioned an Occitan grammar with the laws of poetry. Its first compiler, Guilhem Molinier, completed the Leys d'amor between 1328 and 1337, and it went through two later redactions. By 1471 the academy was losing its Occitan character, and in 1513 it became the Collège de rhétorique et de poésie françaises. In 1554 it gave a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> eglantine to Pierre de Ronsard for his Amours, and in 1694 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> refounded it as the Académie des Jeux Floraux.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Consistori is often credited with encouraging a monotonous poetry, and its poets are judged to have lacked the force of the last troubadours, such as Cerverí de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>. Martí de Riquer criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> school for its narrow themes and strict rules and for exporting occitanisms into Catalan poetry, comparing it to French neoclassicism. Its idea of a gay science later inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-nietzsche">Friedrich Nietzsche</a>'s The Gay Science of 1882.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Consistori del Gay Saber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruin of the southern courts during the Albigensian Crusade scattered the troubadours, yet Toulouse kept the tradition alive: in 1323 a group of its citizens founded the <b>Consistori del Gai Saber</b>, whose poetry contest, the Jeux floraux, still exists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
